{"about":"92968bc0cf42d9652ea12be6542003901e790df25afa74ec9cd14fef2cd65ffd","body":"The claim that preferring highly corroborated theories is consistent with aiming at truth smuggles in induction through the back door by assuming the 'uniformity of nature' or the 'principle of the uniformity of laws.' To assert that a theory which has survived severe tests in the past is a 'better candidate' for being true in the future presupposes that the future will resemble the past regarding the theory's domain. If one strictly rejects induction as Hume and Popper argue, there is no logical warrant to believe that surviving tests yesterday makes a theory more likely to survive tests tomorrow. The preference for the better-corroborated theory is therefore practically useful but logically indefensible on purely deductive grounds, contradicting the conjecture's claim of consistency.\n[would settle: A deductive proof showing that the survival of a theory under past tests increases its fitness for future tests without invoking any assumption about the stability of natural laws or the resemblance of the future to the past.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}